      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Introduction</h3>In the previous text we learned how to locate any point on a sphere using three numbers: a radius <b>r</b> and two angles, theta (<b>θ</b>) and phi (<b>ϕ</b>). Think of it this way: the radius <b>r</b> tells us how far the point is from the center, theta (<b>θ</b>) tells us which way to turn around the sphere, and phi (<b>ϕ</b>) tells us how far to move up or down from the top (<htcite0>).</p><p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: are these the coordinates we use on our smartphone?</b><br /><br />When we open a map application on a smartphone, it shows our position on the planet. Are the spherical coordinates — the radius and the two angles theta and phi — the same coordinates the smartphone uses to tell us where we are, or does it use something different? Keep your guess: we will check it in the conclusion.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "0dd6b53f-ea68-475d-9b18-99d38a428c0a",
                     "text": "History Tracers Atlas",
                     "page": "Where are we on a sphere?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
