      {
         "id": "SECTION_local_content",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgMath13\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgMath13', '0%')\" src=\"\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: The Equator parallel and the Greenwich Meridian cross each other at point O — the reference point we will use throughout this text © Mapswire (<htcite0>)</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "10cd855b-c2dc-4045-8a6c-f8486ef67cfa",
                     "text": "Mapswire",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><h3>Surface Coordinates</h3>The figure in this section shows where the <b>Equator parallel</b> (the imaginary circle halfway between the North and South Poles) and the <i>Greenwich Meridian</i> (the imaginary line passing through Greenwich, England) cross each other. The crossing point is marked as point <b>O</b>. From now on, point O is the reference point for every measurement of latitude and longitude.</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>The origin</h4><p>To locate a point on Earth's surface, we start with the two angular coordinates we already know, theta (<b>θ</b>) and phi (<b>ϕ</b>). In everyday language, however, these angles have other names: theta (<b>θ</b>) is called <b>longitude</b>, and phi (<b>ϕ</b>) is called <b>colatitude</b> — the angle measured from the North Pole.</p><p>Geography adds one more measure, <b>latitude</b> (<b>δ</b>), which tells us how far north or south of the Equator a point is. Latitude and colatitude are simple partners: ϕ = 90° - δ. So a point at latitude 30° N has a colatitude of 90° - 30° = 60°.</p><p>This is only the first of the small simplifications we will meet along the way (<htcite0>; <htcite1>; <htcite2>; <htcite3>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "006b21c9-6800-4deb-bb3f-a367dd38b703",
                     "text": "NOAA",
                     "page": "Longitude",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "06",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72ea1365-bb46-4036-a9e2-7384896e0a28",
                     "text": "NOAA",
                     "page": "Latitude",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "06",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "98aee156-df44-4def-a664-a7db2131c33d",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a7fe67e6-2487-4463-b09e-82de4a04b46c",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
