                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>Longitude</h4>In mathematics, longitude (<b>θ</b>) is expressed with values from 0 to 2π radians, or from 0 to 360 degrees. For geographic coordinates, we split the Earth into two halves of 180 degrees each. The line that separates them is the <b>Greenwich Meridian</b>, fixed in <htdate0> as the zero meridian at the Royal Observatory in Greenwich, England. Every place east of Greenwich is measured as X degrees <b>East</b>, and every place west of it as X degrees <b>West</b>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "006b21c9-6800-4deb-bb3f-a367dd38b703",
                     "text": "NOAA",
                     "page": "Longitude",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "06",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "1894",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": "."
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 340 400\" role=\"img\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\" style=\"width:100%; height:auto; max-width:460px; display:block; margin:0 auto;\" aria-label=\"Sphere showing the Greenwich Meridian at zero degrees, with longitudes measured toward the East and toward the West\"><defs><radialGradient id=\"htLonGlobe\" cx=\"35%\" cy=\"30%\" r=\"75%\"><stop offset=\"0%\" stop-color=\"#eaf3fb\"/><stop offset=\"60%\" stop-color=\"#b9d5ec\"/><stop offset=\"100%\" stop-color=\"#7fa8c9\"/></radialGradient></defs><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"190\" r=\"145\" fill=\"url(#htLonGlobe)\" stroke=\"#2c5d8f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"170\" cy=\"190\" rx=\"145\" ry=\"34\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7a7a7a\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"/><path d=\"M 170 45 A 55 145 0 0 1 170 335\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8fb8da\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M 170 45 A 55 145 0 0 0 170 335\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#8fb8da\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M 170 45 A 110 145 0 0 1 170 335\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a86bb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M 170 45 A 110 145 0 0 0 170 335\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#4a86bb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"170\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"335\" stroke=\"#c0392b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M 170 85 Q 208 68 246 85\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 4\"/><text x=\"208\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"17\" font-style=\"italic\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#333\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke=\"#f4f9fd\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">θ</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#c0392b\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke=\"#f4f9fd\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">0°</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke=\"#f4f9fd\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">East</text><text x=\"255\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke=\"#f4f9fd\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">0° → 180°E</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"20\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke=\"#f4f9fd\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">West</text><text x=\"85\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2f6fb3\" paint-order=\"stroke\" stroke=\"#f4f9fd\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\">0° → 180°W</text></svg><b>Figure 2</b>: A sphere showing the imaginary lines of longitude. The <b>Greenwich Meridian</b> is the reference longitude (0°). Longitudes are measured from it toward the <b>East</b> (0° to 180°E) and toward the <b>West</b> (0° to 180°W).</p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<h4>Latitude</h4>Latitude (<b>δ</b>) is represented mathematically with values from −π/2 to π/2 radians, or from −90° to 90°. The values from 0° to 90° describe only the unsigned magnitude north or south of the Equator. In geographic terms, Earth is divided into two 90-degree regions: the Northern Hemisphere and the Southern Hemisphere. In mathematics, however, positions south of the Equator get negative values. Unlike longitude, which is measured from a prime meridian, latitude is measured from the Equator — the imaginary line that divides the planet into northern and southern halves. Positions north of the Equator are written as \"X degrees <b>North</b>\", and positions south as \"X degrees <b>South</b>\"",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72ea1365-bb46-4036-a9e2-7384896e0a28",
                     "text": "NOAA",
                     "page": "Latitude",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "06",
                        "day": "16"
                     }
                  },
                  {
