      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nNow we can answer the question from the introduction: the coordinates on our smartphone are **not** a different system. They are the same spherical coordinates we already know, with a few small changes. On the Earth's surface the radius never changes, so we can drop it; theta (θ) becomes **longitude**, measured from the Greenwich Meridian; and phi (ϕ) is the **colatitude**, the angle measured from the North Pole; latitude (δ) is therefore δ = 90° − ϕ, measured from the Equator.\n\nSo latitude and longitude are just geographic names for spherical coordinates. The smartphone reads a pair of angles — X degrees East or West, and X degrees North or South — and turns them into the position we see on the map. Look at any point on a map application: behind the blue dot there are always two numbers, one of each kind. The same connection appears in other History Tracers texts: whenever we work with a sphere — a planet, the sky or a globe — spherical coordinates come back with small adjustments of origin, name and range.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
